preConditionHook:
// AN EXTENDED QUIET PERIOD: at least minQuietBars of the lookback bars BEFORE
// this one had a high-low range no larger than smallRangeFraction of the
// current N-bar ATR. Brooks's '30 to 60 minutes of quiet' before an unusually
// large breakout bar, counted in bars, so the timeframe has to be fixed when
// the grid is set.
//
// This is the QUIET half only. The source claim is a conjunction -- a long
// quiet period AND an outsized breakout bar, which should not be entered
// directly -- and the second half is already in the corpus as
// BarRangeAtrMultiple at rangeMultiple = 2.0. The panes AND entry rules, so
// two placements are strictly better than one compound.
//
// The window is bars 1..lookback, EXCLUDING the current bar, because the
// quiet period is what PRECEDES the bar being judged -- which is also what
// lets this rule be ANDed with a current-bar size rule without the breakout
// bar polluting its own precondition. That is the one difference from
// LargeBarCountInWindow's bars 0..lookback-1.
//
// It is NOT LargeBarCountInWindow negated: negating that rule gives 'fewer
// than minCount large bars', which is not 'at least minQuietBars small ones'.
//
// ONE ATR VALUE FOR THE WHOLE WINDOW, read at the current bar. The count is
// an explicit loop rather than CountIF precisely so the ATR is not
// re-evaluated at each counted offset -- CountIF's argument is a true SERIES
// and would shift the function call with it, and a history read of
// WFSafe_AvgTrueRange has no register row.
//
// MultiWalk's WFSafe_AvgTrueRange is a ROLLING accumulator, not a fresh
// N-term mean: seeded with a full sum on the study's first calculated bar,
// thereafter S += TrueRange - TrueRange[Length], re-seeding when the length
// changes. Same block as AtrProfitTarget and LargeBarCountInWindow, including
// the TWO separate statements -- EasyLanguage evaluates
// Summation[1] + Price - Price[Length] left to right, and folding it to
// S += in - out rounds differently and lands on the other side of a
// threshold. The accumulator stays in the hook, which the generator emits
// unconditionally: EL does not short-circuit, so a stateful call in a
// condition would advance every bar in EL and only on some bars here.
//
// quietCount is a class member and persists across bars, so it is reset
// first. Products, not quotients -- EL raises a runtime divide-by-zero.
if (atrLenState != (int)atrLookback) {
    atrSum = 0.0;
    for (int i = 0; i < (int)atrLookback; ++i) {
        double hi = high[i] > close[i + 1] ? high[i] : close[i + 1];
        double lo = low[i] < close[i + 1] ? low[i] : close[i + 1];
        atrSum += hi - lo;
    }
    atrLenState = (int)atrLookback;
} else {
    const int out = (int)atrLookback;
    double hiIn = high[0] > close[1] ? high[0] : close[1];
    double loIn = low[0] < close[1] ? low[0] : close[1];
    double hiOut = high[out] > close[out + 1] ? high[out] : close[out + 1];
    double loOut = low[out] < close[out + 1] ? low[out] : close[out + 1];
    atrSum += hiIn - loIn;
    atrSum -= hiOut - loOut;
}
atrValue = atrSum / (int)atrLookback;
quietCount = 0.0;
for (int k = 1; k <= (int)lookback; ++k) {
    if (el_le(high[k] - low[k], smallRangeFraction * atrValue)) {
        quietCount = quietCount + 1;
    }
}
